{"builder":"Goal: one pull request that meets every acceptance line of issue https://github.com/o/strutil/issues/7:\n- index.js exports padLeft(s, width, fill), which pads s on the left with fill (a space when fill is not given) to width characters\n- padLeft returns s unchanged when s is already width characters or longer\n- README.md has a section for every function index.js exports\n- npm test passes\n\nContext: the repository is an ES module (package.json \"type\": \"module\"). index.js currently exports capitalize, slugify and truncate. README.md has `## capitalize(s)` and `## truncate(s, n)` sections, but no section for slugify. The tests live in test/strutil.test.js and run with `npm test` (`node --test`).\n\nSteps:\n1. In index.js, add `export function padLeft(s, width, fill = ' ')` with a one-line doc comment like the other functions. It returns s padded on the left with fill until it is width characters long, and returns s unchanged when s.length >= width. `s.padStart(width, fill)` does this.\n2. In README.md, add a `## padLeft(s, width, fill)` section that says what it does, that fill defaults to a space, that s comes back unchanged when it is already width characters or longer, and gives the example padLeft('42', 5, '0') -> '00042'.\n3. In README.md, also add the missing `## slugify(s)` section (lower case, words joined by dashes, leading and trailing dashes removed). Every function index.js exports must have its own `## name(...)` heading. Keep the existing sections and the Tests section.\n4. In test/strutil.test.js, import padLeft and add tests for: '42', 5, '0' -> '00042'; '42', 5 -> '   42' (default space); 'abc', 3, '0' -> 'abc'; '12345', 3, '0' -> '12345'.\n5. Run `npm test` and make sure it reports `# fail 0`. Then open the PR.","verifier":"Run every check at the PR head from the repository root. Each check is PASS or FAIL.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"index.js exports padLeft(s, width, fill), which pads s on the left with fill (a space when fill is not given) to width characters\"\nRun:\nnode --input-type=module -e \"\nimport { padLeft } from './index.js'; import assert from 'node:assert/strict';\nassert.equal(typeof padLeft, 'function');\nassert.equal(padLeft('42', 5, '0'), '00042');\nassert.equal(padLeft('42', 5), '   42');\nassert.equal(padLeft('abc', 4, '*'), '*abc');\nassert.equal(padLeft('', 3, '0'), '000');\nconsole.log('OK');\"; echo exit=$?\nPASS: it prints OK and exit=0. FAIL: any import error (padLeft not exported), any assertion error, or a nonzero exit.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"padLeft returns s unchanged when s is already width characters or longer\"\nRun:\nnode --input-type=module -e \"\nimport { padLeft } from './index.js'; import assert from 'node:assert/strict';\nassert.equal(padLeft('12345', 3, '0'), '12345');\nassert.equal(padLeft('abc', 3, '0'), 'abc');\nassert.equal(padLeft('abcd', 3), 'abcd');\nconsole.log('OK');\"; echo exit=$?\nPASS: it prints OK and exit=0. FAIL: any assertion error, an import error, or a nonzero exit.\n\nCheck 3. Expect line: \"README.md has a section for every function index.js exports\"\nRun this. It lists the exported functions and checks each one has a README heading (## or deeper) that starts with its name:\nnode --input-type=module -e \"\nimport * as m from './index.js'; import fs from 'node:fs';\nconst md = fs.readFileSync('README.md','utf8');\nconst heads = md.split('\\n').filter(l => /^#{2,}\\s/.test(l));\nconst fns = Object.keys(m).filter(k => typeof m[k] === 'function');\nconst missing = fns.filter(f => !heads.some(h => new RegExp('^#{2,}\\\\\\\\s+\\`?' + f + '\\\\\\\\b').test(h)));\nconsole.log('exports:', fns.join(', ')); console.log(missing.length ? 'MISSING: ' + missing.join(', ') : 'ALL DOCUMENTED');\"\nThen open README.md and check that each listed export's heading has text under it that describes that function.\nPASS: it prints ALL DOCUMENTED and every exported function (this includes slugify and padLeft as well as capitalize and truncate) has a README section describing it. FAIL: it prints MISSING: followed by any name, or a matched heading has no describing text under it. Before this issue, slugify had no README section, so look at it closely.\n\nCheck 4. Expect line: \"npm test passes\"\nRun: npm test; echo exit=$?\nPASS: exit=0 and the output reports `# fail 0`. FAIL: a nonzero exit or any failing test.\n\nAdvisory (not part of PASS/FAIL):\n- See whether test/strutil.test.js now has tests for padLeft, covering the default fill and the case where s is already long enough.\n- Look at what padLeft does when fill has more than one character or width is not a number. The expect lines do not cover this."}